q("ag-204", C, 3, GA, "Tube X turned milky in 30 seconds. Tube Y took 2 minutes. One tube had air from the lungs blown in and the other had fresh air pumped in at the same rate. Which conclusion is correct?", "X had air from the lungs, because that air has more carbon dioxide", [W("Y had air from the lungs, because that air has more carbon dioxide", "swap", "More carbon dioxide turns limewater milky faster, which is X."), W("X had fresh air, because fresh air has more oxygen", "wrong-idea", "Oxygen does not turn limewater milky."), W("Both had the same gas, because both turned milky", "wrong-idea", "The speeds differ, so the amounts differ.")], ["The faster change means more carbon dioxide."], lambda: tubes([(80, "X", 6, "after 30 s"), (210, "Y", 6, "after 2 min")], "Limewater in two tubes"))
TEX = tb(["Gas", "At rest (%)", "After running (%)"], [["Oxygen in exhaled air", "16", "14"], ["Carbon dioxide in exhaled air", "4", "6"]], [190, 100, 130], "Exhaled air of a boy")
q("ag-205", C, 3, RD, "A boy's exhaled air was tested at rest and after running. What does the table show?", "His body used more oxygen and made more carbon dioxide when he ran", [W("His body used the same oxygen but made more carbon dioxide when he ran", "mixed", "Less oxygen in exhaled air means more was used."), W("His body made oxygen when he ran", "reversed", "Oxygen in exhaled air went down."), W("The air he breathed in had more carbon dioxide", "wrong-idea", "The table is about exhaled air only.")], ["Exhaled oxygen is lower and exhaled carbon dioxide is higher after running."], TEX)
JG = lambda: graph(["0", "1", "2", "3", "4"], [("X", [21, 19, 18, 17, 16]), ("Y", [0.04, 1, 2, 3, 4])], 25, "Time (h)", "Gas in jar (%)", "A mouse in a closed jar", ystep=5, legend="tr")
q("ag-206", C, 3, RD, "A mouse is sealed in a jar of air. The graph shows two gases in the jar. Which gas does line Y stand for?", "Carbon dioxide, which the mouse gives out", [W("Oxygen, which the mouse gives out", "swap", "The mouse uses oxygen, so oxygen is X."), W("Nitrogen, which the mouse gives out", "wrong-gas", "Nitrogen does not change much."), W("Carbon dioxide, which the mouse takes in", "reversed", "The mouse gives out carbon dioxide.")], ["Y goes up from a tiny amount, so it is carbon dioxide from respiration."], JG)
q("ag-207", C, 3, GA, "A mouse is sealed in a jar. It survives for a longer time when a green plant in bright light is also in the jar. Why?", "The plant adds oxygen to the air in the jar", [W("The plant adds nitrogen to the air in the jar", "wrong-gas", "Mice do not use nitrogen."), W("The plant takes in oxygen and gives out carbon dioxide in light", "reversed", "In light the plant makes food and gives out oxygen."), W("The plant takes up space, so the mouse needs less air", "wrong-idea", "Taking up space does not give more oxygen.")], ["Plants give out oxygen in light."], None)
q("ag-208", C, 3, GA, "A green plant in a sealed box is moved from bright light into a cupboard with no light. Which change in the gases of the box is expected?", "Oxygen goes down as the plant makes none in the dark", [W("Oxygen goes up because the cupboard is cooler", "wrong-idea", "Without light the plant does not add oxygen."), W("Nitrogen goes down because the plant uses it up", "wrong-gas", "Plants do not use up nitrogen in this way."), W("Nothing changes because plants do not respire", "wrong-idea", "Plants respire and use oxygen.")], ["In the dark the plant uses oxygen in respiration and adds none."], None)
q("ag-209", C, 3, GA, "A burning candle is lowered into a jar that has been filled with only carbon dioxide. The flame goes out. Which statement explains this?", "Carbon dioxide does not support burning", [W("Carbon dioxide takes the oxygen out of the candle wax", "wrong-idea", "Carbon dioxide does not remove oxygen from wax."), W("Carbon dioxide cools the flame, so it cannot burn", "wrong-idea", "The reason is that the gas cannot support burning."), W("Carbon dioxide is a liquid in the jar", "wrong-idea", "It is a gas in the jar.")], ["Without oxygen, the flame cannot continue."], None)
q("ag-210", C, 3, GA, "A candle goes out in a closed jar while the graph shows 16% oxygen is still there. Which statement is correct?", "The oxygen left was too little to keep the candle burning", [W("All the oxygen was used up, so the graph must be wrong", "wrong-idea", "Some oxygen remained."), W("The candle needs no oxygen when the amount is below 21%", "wrong-idea", "A flame needs enough oxygen."), W("The remaining gas was carbon dioxide only", "wrong-idea", "Most of the rest was nitrogen.")], ["A flame goes out when oxygen falls too low, even if some remains."], O2G)
